# check-secrets denylist — EXAMPLE
#
# Copy this to `check-secrets.denylist` (git-ignored) and put YOUR real
# identifiers in it. `./check-secrets.sh` fails the scan if any of them appear
# in a tracked file.
#
# One pattern per line. # starts a comment. Patterns are grep -E (case-insensitive),
# so `example\.com` matches example.com literally and `\bargus\b` is a whole word.
#
# Why a separate file: the list of your hostnames and surnames must not be
# committed — otherwise the tripwire becomes the leak.
#
# --- what to include -------------------------------------------------------
# - your domain(s)                       e.g.  yourdomain\.com
# - machine / host nicknames             e.g.  \bmybox\b  \bhetzner1\b
# - usernames and service accounts       e.g.  \bdeploy\b
# - storage/pool/dataset names           e.g.  \bvaultpool\b
# - VPN/tailnet identifiers              e.g.  tail1a2b3c
# - surnames / household names           e.g.  \bsmith\b
# - any LAN subnet you actually use      e.g.  10\.69\.1\.
# - service account emails               e.g.  ops@yourdomain\.com
#
# --- examples (harmless placeholders — replace with your own) --------------
# yourdomain\.com
# \bhost1\b
# \bmyhost\b
# 192\.168\.50\.
# tail1234abcd